<section class="phero"><div class="phero-media"><img src="/assets/img/photos/colonial-home.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/colonial-home-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/colonial-home.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="800" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><a href="/new-hampshire-cities/">Cities</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Derry</span></nav><p class="kicker">Merrimack Valley · Rockingham County</p><h1>Insurance Appraiser in Derry, NH</h1><p class="lede">Derry is one of the state’s largest towns, a commuter place of cul-de-sacs and older village centers threaded with woods. When a storm comes through, the trees come down – and the claim argument usually starts with the roof.</p><div class="btns"><a class="btn btn-gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line-light" href="/contact-nh-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><ul class="phero-chips"><li><small>County</small><b>Rockingham County</b></li><li><small>Region</small><b>Merrimack Valley</b></li><li><small>Housing</small><b>colonial-style subdivisions, older village homes and condo complexes</b></li></ul></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<div class="local-card"><div><h2 class="local-t">At a glance</h2><p><b>Areas we cover:</b> Derry Village, East Derry, downtown Derry, the Beaver Lake area.</p><p><b>Storm history:</b> The October 2017 windstorm knocked out power to a large share of New Hampshire overnight, and wooded Rockingham County towns like Derry dealt with downed trees across roofs, decks and driveways.</p></div><ul class="chips"><li>Windstorms</li><li>Hail</li><li>Ice dams</li><li>Falling trees</li><li>Basement water</li></ul></div>

<h2>A big town that still has woods in the backyard</h2>
<p>Derry grew fast from the 1970s on, as I-93 made it an easy commute. That growth left a landscape of colonial-style subdivisions on wooded lots, condo and townhouse complexes, and older housing around Derry Village and East Derry, where the town’s history goes back much further. Downtown along Broadway has 19th-century buildings and older multifamily houses. Around Beaver Lake you’ll find seasonal cottages turned year-round homes.</p>
<h2>The roof is usually the first argument</h2>
<p>Most Derry subdivisions were roofed in waves, which means a lot of homes are reaching the end of their original shingles at the same time. After a windstorm or hailstorm, insurers often point to age. Sometimes the damage is plainly new – creased and missing tabs on the windward side, torn ridge caps, fresh granule loss. Sometimes it is mixed.</p>
<p>Questions that come up over and over:</p>
<ul>
<li>Should the repair be a few shingles, a slope or the whole roof?</li>
<li>Can replacement shingles reasonably match, or is the roof effectively a new color patch?</li>
<li>Has the estimate included drip edge, ice-and-water shield and other items a code-compliant reroof needs?</li>
<li>How much depreciation was withheld, and what do you have to do to recover it?</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/nh-appraisal-services/roof-and-hail-appraisal/">roof appraisal</a> page explains how those are evaluated. You can sanity-check roof size with the <a href="/nh-claim-calculators/roof-squares-pitch-tool/">roof squares and pitch tool</a>.</p>
<h2>Trees, decks and outbuildings</h2>
<p>Wooded lots are part of Derry’s appeal, and a windstorm turns them into claims. A single oak can take out a roof section, a deck, a fence and a shed. Policies treat those differently: the dwelling, other structures and tree removal often carry separate limits. When the estimate splits the job into pieces that each look reasonable alone but don’t add up to a repair, appraisal can put it back together. See <a href="/nh-appraisal-services/falling-tree-claim-appraisal/">falling-tree appraisal</a>.</p>
<h2>Water in finished basements</h2>
<p>Many Derry homes have finished lower levels, and spring thaws or heavy rain bring water in. Whether you are covered depends on where the water came from – surface flooding, groundwater, a sump failure, a sewer backup or a broken pipe each fall under different provisions or endorsements. Settle the source before you argue about the price.</p>
<p>Derry Village and East Derry tell a different story from the subdivisions. Homes there can date to the 18th and 19th centuries, with timber frames, fieldstone foundations and additions from every era since. A loss in one of these houses calls for an estimator who understands that a “simple” wall repair may involve plaster, horsehair, irregular framing and trim that hasn’t been made in a hundred years.</p>
<h2>Working with us in Derry</h2>
<p>New Hampshire Insurance Appraiser reviews estimates, represents policyholders in appraisal and serves as umpire. We don’t give legal advice; for coverage questions, talk to a New Hampshire attorney. Use the <a href="/nh-claim-calculators/gap-and-fee-estimator/">gap and fee estimator</a> or <a href="/contact-nh-appraiser/">contact us</a> to start.</p>

<h2>Appraisal help available in Derry</h2>
<p>Every New Hampshire Insurance Appraiser service is available in Derry and the surrounding towns, including:</p>
<ul><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/appraisal-clause-representation-nh/">Policyholder’s Appraiser on the Panel</a></li><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/homeowners-claim-appraisal-nh/">Single-Family Home Claims</a></li><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/nh-commercial-hoa-appraisal/">Condo, Association &amp; Business Claims</a></li><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/roof-and-hail-appraisal/">Roof Damage &amp; Hail Disputes</a></li><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/wind-microburst-tornado-appraisal/">Microburst &amp; Windstorm Losses</a></li><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/tropical-storm-hurricane-appraisal-nh/">Tropical Remnants &amp; Coastal Storms</a></li></ul>
<h2>Asked by Derry owners</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>My insurer says my roof was just old. Can I still go to appraisal?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>If the insurer has accepted that some storm damage occurred and the dispute is how much repair is needed, appraisal is often available. If it denies there was any covered damage, that is a coverage dispute.</p></div></details><details><summary>A tree hit my shed and my house. Are those covered the same way?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Usually not. Many policies cover detached structures under a separate “other structures” limit. Tree removal often has its own limit too. Check your declarations page.</p></div></details></div>
